17] [17592186347936 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347936 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347936 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347936 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347936 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186346698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347936 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bcca5e50-34f9-509a-ae72-dfeccfa1ac60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348240 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348240 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79570" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348240 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348240 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=45\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed and has a damping coefficient $c=20\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters) and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348240 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "935f78ae-2339-577c-affb-d269b5e06297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348240 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n5{y}^{″}=-20{y}^{′}-45y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354753 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354753 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354753 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "098be5a2-3449-53d3-8787-924e2faef4a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354765 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921