$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357720 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb97ba47-964f-5f2d-8d82-4fa760cb4511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357720 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+5y=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357733 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357733 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357733 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357733 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357733 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2fac86fa-e6ef-53c4-af18-2a5008f32591" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357733 #edb/ref 1074 "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=1\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation 